    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Miscommunication occurs often in conversation. Humans employ various repair strategies to resolve miscommunications that occur in human-to-human speech, in what is called “conversational repair”. However, most chatbots currently in use either do not engage in repair at all, or conduct repair in ways that are, to a human speaker, clumsy and unnatural. This unnatural repair may lead to negative user experiences. This thesis draws on research into users’ experience of bots and conversational repair to explore the types of miscommunication, and possible repair strategies, that may occur between a task-oriented survey chatbot and a user. I find that errors in misunderstanding and repetition are common for medical survey chatbots, but that the first difficult key step for the chatbot is to notice that an error has occurred. From there, I identify repair strategies that are most ‘natural’ in conversation, including previously researched techniques such as “Confirmation”, along with new techniques such as “Simplification” and “Sense-Making”, in the hopes of building chatbots that will be able to have more positive interactions with users. This naturalistic repair, where a patient and the chatbot are able to continue a conversation naturally and fluidly in a way that is metaphorically similar to a tango, is key to improving user experience with chatbots.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
